PART II -- THE RE-CAPTURE AT THE FINAL INSTRUMENT (THIS IS THE DISCHARGE)
================================================================================

BUILD IDENTITY. Server binary and soak_harness bench binary were produced by
`cargo build --release --bin topgun-server --bench soak_harness` over the final,
clean tree. Their link times are 2026-08-08T08:37:57Z (server) and
2026-08-08T08:48:33Z (bench) -- a 636s gap, which EXCEEDS the runner's own 600s
window, so the branch that fires on this pair is the runner's "probably NOT from
one build" WARNING.

CORRECTION. This block previously asserted the two binaries were provably built
together, and the companion EG-1 artifact quoted a 60s gap in support. That
assertion is WITHDRAWN and the 60s figure was wrong; `spec356a-eager-registration.log`
Part II carries the full correction, including where the false number came from.
Nothing in this Part ever depended on same-build identity. What it needs is that
the SOURCE ARTIFACT came from a POST-FIX instrument, and that is carried by two
facts that are not same-build claims: both link times postdate the
licensed-backlog repair of 2026-08-04 by four days, and the eligible_refs
max=1000 fingerprint recorded in `spec356a-eager-registration.log` Part II is
unreachable by the pre-fix code.

The source artifact for this fixture is the post-repair smoke `prune.csv` -- the
same file whose census discharges EG-1 in `spec356a-eager-registration.log`
Part II, so the two gates are still cut from ONE RUN of ONE instrument rather
than from two runs of two. That is a statement about one runner invocation, and
is untouched by the withdrawal above.

Note what this path does NOT depend on, so the reader does not over-read the
rebuild: the fixture path starts no harness and never resolves either binary --
it is entered, validated and executed before the runner reaches its binary
resolution. The rebuild matters because the SOURCE ARTIFACT had to come from the
final instrument, not because this path executes any of it. The same structure
is why the build-gap WARNING above is inert for THIS gate in particular: the
runner branch that would emit it is never reached on the fixture path.

